    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d847">

                <pb facs="0841" n="527" sameAs="frus:frus1914Supp/pg527"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 841.731/140</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Page</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <dateline>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Embassy</hi>, <lb/>
                    <placeName>
                        <hi rendition="#italic">London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                        when="1914-12-17">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">December 17, 1914</hi>.</date>
                    <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received 7:30 p.m.</hi>] </dateline>
                <p rendition="#center">[Telegram]</p>
                <p>Your 614, November 24, censorship of commercial cables.<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d847-f1"/> British Government informs me that arrangements are being
                    made to allow the use of Meyer’s Atlantic cotton code, 39th edition, in foreign
                    telegrams and that a public announcement on the subject will be made as soon as
                    the date of the admission of the code is settled. It is not at present
                    practicable to allow the use of more than one cotton code and British Government
                    are informed that the 39th edition of Meyer’s code is the one most generally
                    used.</p>
                <closer>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Ambassador</hi>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
